    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d50">

                <pb facs="0134" n="968" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg968"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 600.119/561</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-01T22:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 1, 1918, 10 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received March 1, 7 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8865. War Trade Board [from Sheldon]:</p>
                <p>No. 216. Your 113.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d50-f1"/> Article 2. You mention
                    representatives in northern neutrals of United States and Great Britain only. Is
                    it not your intention that these local joint committees shall comprise also
                    French and Italian representatives and that the joint plan as per your 113 shall
                    later apply to all the Allies rather than just Great Britain and the United
                    States? I should strongly approve extending it to the four cobelligerents.
                    Section 6. Do you intend me to become officially a member of the existing
                    Contraband Committee? Your reference to this in section 7 leads me to think that
                    you do. I should recommend that an inter-Allied blockade committee be set up as
                    soon as possible, but would suggest that it be called, say, inter-Allied export
                    control committee as a more palatable name to the northern neutrals. The Foreign
                    Office agree to the general plan outlined in your No. 113.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
